\details{
\strong{Subsumed by R itself.} The matrix definition commands
exist because TDA has no other way to get a matrix into a session;
R does. \code{mdef} (literal) is \code{matrix()}; \code{mdefc}
(constant) is \code{matrix(d, m, n)}; \code{mdefi} (rectangular
identity) is \code{diag(1, m, n)}; \code{mdeff} (from file) is
\code{as.matrix(read.table(...))} or \code{\link{tda_read_table}};
\code{mpr} (print a matrix expression) is printing the return value
of any matrix wrapper. The expression operators of section 5.2.5
-- \code{nd}, \code{ndi}, \code{td}, \code{chd}, \code{fd},
\code{mr}, \code{ndf}, \code{poisson}, \code{negbin}, \code{bivn},
\code{jul}, \code{july}, \code{julm}, \code{juld} and relatives --
are \code{pnorm}, \code{qnorm}, \code{pt}, \code{pchisq},
\code{pf}, the Mill's ratio \code{dnorm(x)/(1 - pnorm(x))},
\code{dnorm}, \code{dpois}/\code{dnbinom} (TDA returns the log
density), \code{mvtnorm::pmvnorm}, and R's date arithmetic. The
engine's implementations of all of these are compared against
R's on every run of the differential fuzzer, so using either side
gives the same numbers; to evaluate one of TDA's operators
directly, pass the expression to \code{\link{tda_evalf}}.

\strong{Delivered by another wrapper.} \code{mdefg} (adjacency
matrix from the current graph) is
\code{tda_g_edges(g, format = "full_square")}, with \code{-1}
marking absent edges. \code{mbrr} is \code{\link{tda_brr}}.
\code{mple} is \code{\link{tda_mple}}. \code{mlp}'s raw-tableau
form is assembled internally by \code{\link{tda_mlp}} from the
objective, constraints, and bounds.

\strong{Reached through the session they belong to.}
\code{mdefb} (a block of the data matrix) requires a
\code{dblock} context and is used through the \code{commands=}
argument of \code{\link{tda_dblock}}. \code{mplog},
\code{mppar}, \code{mpcov} and \code{mpgrad} are parameters OF
estimation commands, not commands; their contents are what the
fitted objects already carry as \code{$logLik}, \code{$estimates}
and \code{$vcov} (case-specific gradients, \code{mpgrad}, can be
requested through a command's \code{options=} where supported).


